# POSIX Shell Script, `.sh` Schema
#
# `entrypoint.sh` is the standard entrypoint of the dev base image: the program
# the container starts, and the program every image layered on the base
# inherits. It is deliberately small — it validates the run contract, prepares
# the workspace, and hands control to the harness.
#
# It is installed as `/usr/local/bin/agent-entrypoint` (see
# `Containerfile.schema`), root-owned and mode 0755.

## File Format

`#!/bin/sh` plus POSIX shell. No bashisms: the script must run on the smallest
shell the base image ships, because it is the one file every layer depends on.
`sh -n <file>` is the syntax check.

## Contract

| Aspect | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Invocation | Container start: `ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/agent-entrypoint"]`. A layer inherits this and changes only `AGENT_HARNESS`. |
| Inputs (environment) | `AGENT_HARNESS` (required; name or absolute path of the harness CLI), `AGENT_WORKSPACE_DIR` (default `/workspace`), `AGENT_ID` (default the container's hostname). |
| Inputs (arguments) | Every argument passed to the container, forwarded to the harness unchanged. |
| Inputs (filesystem) | `AGENT_WORKSPACE_DIR` — created if absent, required to be writable. |
| Output | The harness process, with `AGENT_ID` and `AGENT_WORKSPACE_DIR` exported, as PID 1. |
| Exit codes | `78` on any refusal, before anything starts; otherwise the harness's own exit status, unchanged. |
| Side effects | Creates the workspace directory if it does not exist. Nothing else — no file, no port, no daemon. |

## Patterns Used, and Why

- **`set -eu`** — an unset variable or an unhandled failure aborts, rather than
  letting the script fall through with an empty workspace path or an empty
  harness name.
- **One `refuse()` function** — every refusal is the same shape: one line on
  stderr naming the offending variable or path, exit 78. A consumer can test
  "did it refuse?" with the exit status and "why?" with the message, without
  parsing.
- **`case` validation before any action** — the harness name, the workspace
  path, and the agent id are all checked before a single process is started or
  directory created, so a bad run leaves nothing behind.
- **`command -v` for the harness** — the contract is "the CLI found in PATH",
  so the lookup goes through the shell's own resolution, and an absolute path
  works through the same call.
- **`exec`** — the harness replaces this script, so it *is* PID 1: the
  container runtime's stop signal reaches it directly, the exit status is its
  own, and there is no wrapper process to confuse a supervisor or a
  process-tree walk.
- **`AGENT_ID` is charset-restricted** (`[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*`, ≤64
  characters) — the id is a name in other components (workspace labels, state
  directories), so path separators, whitespace, and control characters are
  refused at the one place that can refuse them cheaply.
- **Deterministic refusal for a read-only workspace** — there is no repair
  path, because repair needs root, and this image has none (R-1). A clear
  refusal beats a harness that starts and fails on its first write.

## Validation

```
sh -n entrypoint.sh                                  # syntax only, exits 0
scripts/verify-base-image.sh                         # the whole contract, end to end
```

The end-to-end checks build a throwaway layer image that adds a stub harness,
then assert: PID 1 is the harness, the working directory is the workspace, the
agent id defaults to the hostname and is honoured when set, container arguments
arrive at the harness, the harness's exit status passes through, and each of
the refusals returns 78 with its message.
